=== SECTION A — PROBLEM STATEMENT ===

A1.
This pipeline converts a PDF report into useful insights through five connected stages. First,
the system reads the PDF file and extracts text from each page. Then, the extracted data is 
organized into a structured table containing details like page number, word count, and 
preview text. After that, the processed data is saved in CSV and JSON formats for future use.
The combined text is then sent to the Groq Llm AI model, which generates a short 
three-point summary. Finally, the pipeline creates a bar chart using Matplotlib to visually 
display the word count of each page. Each stage depends on the previous stage’s output.

=== SECTION B — ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM ===

B1.
PDF (./data/report.pdf)
-> [Ingest]    -> list[str]  (raw text per page)
-> [Structure] -> DataFrame  (page_no, word_count, first_50_chars)
-> [Store]     -> output/pages.csv + output/pages.json
-> [Summarise] -> Groq LLM   -> 3 bullet-point summary (text)
-> [Visualise] -> matplotlib -> bar chart (word_count per page)

=== SECTION C — CODE BLOCKS ===

C1. LOGGING SETUP

python
import logging

logging.basicConfig(
    level=logging.INFO,
    format="%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(message)s",
)
logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)


C2. INGEST + EXTRACT

python
import fitz

logger.info("Stage 1: Ingest - opening PDF")
doc = fitz.open("./data/report.pdf")
pages_text = []
for i, page in enumerate(doc):
    text = page.get_text()
    pages_text.append(text)
    logger.info("Extracted page %d (%d chars)", i + 1, len(text))
doc.close()


C3. STRUCTURE

python
import pandas as pd

logger.info("Stage 2: Structure - building DataFrame")
rows = []
for i, text in enumerate(pages_text):
    rows.append({
        "page_no": i + 1,
        "word_count": len(text.split()),
        "first_50_chars": text[:50],
    })
df = pd.DataFrame(rows)


C4. STORE

python
import os

logger.info("Stage 3: Store - writing CSV and JSON")
os.makedirs("output", exist_ok=True)
df.to_csv("output/pages.csv", index=False)
df.to_json("output/pages.json", orient="records")


C5. SUMMARISE WITH GROQ

python
from groq import Groq

logger.info("Stage 4: Summarise - calling Groq")
combined = "".join(pages_text)[:2000]
client = Groq(api_key=os.environ["GROQ_API_KEY"])
response = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="llama-3.1-8b-instant",
    messages=[
        {
            "role": "user",
            "content": f"Summarise this in exactly 3 bullet points.\n\n{combined}",
        }
    ],
)
print(response.choices[0].message.content)


C6. VISUALISE

python
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

logger.info("Stage 5: Visualise - bar chart")
plt.bar(df["page_no"], df["word_count"])
plt.title("Word Count per Page")
plt.xlabel("Page Number")
plt.ylabel("Word Count")
plt.savefig("output/word_count.png")


=== SECTION D — EXPECTED GROQ RESPONSE SHAPE ===

D1.
The Groq call prints a short, plain-text summary formatted as exactly three bullet points.
Each bullet is one concise sentence drawn from the first 2000 characters of the report.
The bullets are likely to cover the report’s main topic, its key findings or figures, and its
conclusion or recommendation. Output is text only - no code, JSON, or tables.

=== SECTION E — LESSONS LEARNED ===

E1.
What worked: 
    The linear stage design kept the data flow obvious - each stage had one job and a
    clear hand-off, so debugging was easy. PyMuPDF and pandas made ingestion and structuring 
    almost trivial, and logging at every stage gave instant visibility into progress.

What was painful:
    Handling the Groq response shape and trimming text to 2000 characters took trial and
    error, and making the output folder overwrite cleanly rather than append needed care.
 
One MLOps practice from Day 6 I would add in production: 
    externalise configuration and secrets (model name, file paths,
    GROQ_API_KEY) into environment variables or a config file, plus structured run logging 
    and output versioning, so every pipeline run is reproducible and traceable.